{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793387686,"nonce":"45DJeYMy+kp5vTVS2rYQnE4YfP8=","title":"LSP display compiles retype the whole import graph for each markup completion","message":"A completion request inside inline markup (42bb750a) compiles the document in a fresh CompilationUnit with the display cursor set, separately from the document's analysis. For a haxelib project the BLADE cache is off, so that unit compiles every import from source again.\n\nMeasured on the NUC (Ashui main 8ab05e0, tests/fixtures/display/MarkupDisplay.hx, 291 typed files):\n- ordinary analysis of the document: 216 s\n- display compile at an attribute list: 273 s; at another, 682 s when it ran alongside the analysis and with a stale stdlib snapshot\n- the same requests in a file importing only HxxTags: under 1 s\n\nSo a markup completion costs about one more full analysis.\n\nWhy a separate unit: a display compile gives build-macro fields named-file positions (getBuildFields wraps them with with_source_positions), so containsDisplayPosition can see each expression's file and end. An ordinary analysis cannot take those spans: they leave the file's ordinary file id, which FileSymbolIndex keys hover and references by.\n\nOptions:\n1. Give ordinary positions (file_id \u003c NAMED_FILE_ID) a file and an end, so containsDisplayPosition works on them. A completion arriving while the document's analysis is pending could then restart that analysis with the cursor set and answer from it: one compile, not two.\n2. Reuse the analysis session's typed imports in the display unit, retyping only the edited file. This needs a way to replace one file's declarations in a live graph.","files":null}]}